<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Hohenheim</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1818">1818</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Hohenheim academy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of Hohenheim is a university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, housed in and around the 18th-century Hohenheim Palace. It was founded in 1818 by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-of-wurttemberg">William I of Württemberg</a> as an agricultural school after the famine of 1816–1817, and it became one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s leading agricultural colleges. It gained university status in 1967 and teaches agricultural, natural and economic sciences.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-hohenheim.jpg" alt="University of Hohenheim" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uni_Hohenheim-Logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The University of Hohenheim lies in the district of Plieningen, on the southern edge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, and is the city's oldest university. Between 1770 and 1794 the Karlsschule had been the only university in Stuttgart. The eruption of the Indonesian volcano <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-tambora">Mount Tambora</a> in 1815 triggered a global climate change and was one of the causes of the famine that struck the Kingdom of Württemberg early in the nineteenth century. In 1818 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-of-wurttemberg">William I of Württemberg</a> set up an Agricultural Academy at Hohenheim to improve nutrition in the kingdom radically through teaching, experiment and demonstration. It began with 18 students and three professors. Its first director was Johann Nepomuk Schwerz, and it was housed in Hohenheim Palace, built by Charles Eugene, Duke of Württemberg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1847 the institution was given the rank of an Academy of Agriculture and Forestry, and in 1904 its name became Agricultural College. It won the right to confer doctorates in 1918 and habilitations in 1919. In 1923 Margarete von Wrangell was appointed to the chair for plant nutrition, becoming the first female full professor at a German university. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">National Socialism</a> the college was brought into line with the party's ideology, and it was forced to close in 1945.</p><p>The college reopened in 1946, having survived the war relatively undamaged. In 1964 the faculties of Agricultural Sciences and Natural Sciences were created, followed in 1968 by the Faculty of Business, Economics and Social Sciences. Hohenheim has had university status since 1967, when it took the name Universität Hohenheim. It is not connected to the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, which was founded in 1829, although the two collaborate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The baroque palace is the university's emblem and main building, and it stands among historic parkland and the botanical Hohenheim Gardens, which include the historic Landesarboretum Baden-Württemberg. The university runs several museums open to the public: the German Agricultural Museum, which has 5,700 square metres of covered exhibition space, the Zoological and Veterinary Museum, housed in the palace, and the Hohenheim History Museum in the Spielhaus, which tells the history of the municipality, the palace and the university. Its research has centred on agriculture, nutrition and environmental protection, and it was a co-founder in 2001 of the Euroleague for Life Sciences, a network of European universities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “University of Hohenheim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Queen Catherine, wife of William I, founded charitable associations and a savings bank during the famine, and in 1818 the king established an agricultural institute at <b>Hohenheim</b> and the agricultural festival at Cannstatt, the origin of the Cannstatter Wasen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
